[
    {
        "content": "<p>I have recently made <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41082\">#41082</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41147\">#41147</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41148\">#41148</a>, as I felt ultimately <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/37631\">#37631</a> was trying to do too many different things.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41082\">#41082</a> - adds Function.diag and a notation for it, so that <code>△(x) = (x, x)</code> (feedback for this notation sought!)<br>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41147\">#41147</a> - refactors across the codebase to use <a href=\"http://Function.prod\">Function.prod</a> everywhere the anonymous form is used.<br>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41148\">#41148</a> - expand lemmas about <a href=\"http://Function.prod\">Function.prod</a></p>\n<p>Currently these are not dependent on one another and I think could merge in any order.</p>\n<p>I have not yet:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Created a PR adding a notation for <a href=\"http://Function.prod\">Function.prod</a>. I continue to believe <code>×ᶠ</code> is a good option.</li>\n<li>Added a definition for \"ofProd\", the function which sends a map to a product into a product of maps, or notation for the same (thoughts on this welcome).</li>\n<li>Changed  the various equivalences that could/should use this.</li>\n<li>Added Function.prodMap as an alternative name for <a href=\"http://Prod.map\">Prod.map</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I would still quite like to do these things but they are not the highest priority right now. (I think we could also sensibly create a Sum-analogue to all of these but that is outside the scope of this discussion).</p>",
        "id": 607343481,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782816399
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>See <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"144837\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/144837-PR-reviews/topic/.2337631.3A.20Function.2Eprod.20and.20related.20definitions/with/607342639\">#PR reviews &gt; #37631: Function.prod and related definitions</a>  for prior discussion.</p>",
        "id": 607343904,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782816554
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Incidentally we are somewhat inconsistent about whether we name this \"prod\" or \"prodMk\" in lemmas - see <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Measurable.prod#doc\">docs#Measurable.prod</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Measurable.prodMk#doc\">docs#Measurable.prodMk</a> - though I think generally speaking it is prod in definitions.</p>",
        "id": 607344340,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782816710
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>\"prodMk\" only refers to <code>Prod.mk</code>, but \"prod\" is also used for other kinds of products, for example <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=MonoidHom.prod#doc\">docs#MonoidHom.prod</a></p>",
        "id": 607347791,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1782817912
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But <a href=\"http://MonoidHom.prod\">MonoidHom.prod</a> is the same as <a href=\"http://Function.prod\">Function.prod</a> (equipped with the proof that etc.), and indeed is the same construction as in Measurable.prodMk - confusingly, Measurable.prodMk is not just about prodMk!</p>",
        "id": 607348499,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782818107
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Indeed if you look at <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Continuous.prodMk_right#doc\">docs#Continuous.prodMk_right</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Continuous.prodMk#doc\">docs#Continuous.prodMk</a> you'll see what I mean.</p>",
        "id": 607348558,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782818126
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>Measurable.prodMk</code> does seem to be about <code>Prod.mk</code>, it's <code>Measurable f → Measurable g → Measurable fun x =&gt; Prod.mk (f x) (g x)</code>. This is the same as other lemmas like <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Continuous.div%E2%82%80#doc\">docs#Continuous.div₀</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Continuous.sqrt#doc\">docs#Continuous.sqrt</a>, where the function is applied to the outputs of other functions instead of directly to the argument.</p>",
        "id": 607359195,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1782821577
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That is true I suppose - but <code>fun x =&gt; Prod.mk (f x) (g x)</code> is <code>Function.prod f g</code>.</p>",
        "id": 607359327,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782821619
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>then, we can also have a version for <code>Function.prod</code>, but we should keep the version for <code>Prod.mk</code> so people can use it if they need a lemma for <code>Prod.mk</code> and not <code>Function.prod</code>.</p>",
        "id": 607360296,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1782821916
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That's fair!</p>",
        "id": 607360357,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782821933
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think we generally speaking want all three of these (and with some experimentation, this seems to give the nicest behaviour for funprop etc.)</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">continuous_???</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Z</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fst</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">snd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">continuous_inf_rng</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">trans</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">continuous_induced_rng</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">and</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">continuous_induced_rng</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">continuous_prod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Z</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">prod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">continuous_???</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">continuous_prodMk</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Z</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↔</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∧</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">continuous_???</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">continuity</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fun_prop</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">prod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Z</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">fst</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Prod</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">snd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">continuous_???</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">mpr</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">h₁</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h₂</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 607361868,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782822351
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Do you have thoughts on what continuous_??? should be called?</p>",
        "id": 607361943,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782822369
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Analogous with <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=continuous_sum_dom#doc\">docs#continuous_sum_dom</a>, perhaps: continuous_prod_rng?</p>",
        "id": 607362220,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782822438
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm surprised we don't already have continuous_???, which is the universal property of the product topology</p>",
        "id": 607363394,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1782822728
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>we can call it <code>continuous_prod_iff</code> or something like that</p>",
        "id": 607363432,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1782822736
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Right - broadly what I am finding is that we do indeed lack this in the universal property form.</p>",
        "id": 607363576,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782822783
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think <code>continuous_prod_iff</code> is not good because <code>continuous_prod</code> should be that (we are very inconsistent about when we use an iff here).</p>",
        "id": 607363740,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782822830
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>continuous_prod_rng is analogous to continuous_inf_rng from which it derives.</p>",
        "id": 607363839,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782822854
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=continuous_sum_dom#doc\">docs#continuous_sum_dom</a> is <em>not</em> <code>continuous_sum_iff</code> which it should be if you follow that logic.</p>",
        "id": 607364637,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782823066
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(btw I also think prodMk_right should be in a more general form, i.e.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">prodMk_right'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fun_prop</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">prod_left_id</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Y</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Continuous</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Function</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">prod</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">id</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fun_prop</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>... but really I think basically there's just a ton of tidying up that can be done here.</p>",
        "id": 607367978,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782823969
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Incidentally, I wish we had a name for the function that maps x to (x, f x) (and to (f x, x)). But I can't think of anything that feels really natura;</p>",
        "id": 607557657,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782895562
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Function.graphEmbed</p>",
        "id": 607582224,
        "sender_full_name": "Yakov Pechersky",
        "timestamp": 1782903026
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>maybe Function.Embedding.graph?</p>",
        "id": 607582373,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782903055
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My personal opinion is that finding functions named via dot notation on the function type is not very discoverable. I was assuming there is a Function.graph which would be the term of type Set X x Y. So having an adjacent name makes it easier to find by completion. But this opinion is very bikesheddy</p>",
        "id": 607582905,
        "sender_full_name": "Yakov Pechersky",
        "timestamp": 1782903245
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Unfortunately Function.graph is a SetRel. I definitely agree that dot notation on the function type is very mixed. I think support here could definitely be improved.</p>",
        "id": 607583064,
        "sender_full_name": "Wrenna Robson",
        "timestamp": 1782903298
    }
]